1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,793
Pool balls split apart at an angle.

2
00:00:02,793 --> 00:00:06,066
Two players collide, and
both are knocked sideways.

3
00:00:06,466 --> 00:00:08,768
Not every hit is a straight line.

4
00:00:08,768 --> 00:00:10,811
Most hits happen at an angle.

5
00:00:11,261 --> 00:00:14,236
Aarav hits a carrom coin,
a little off centre.

6
00:00:14,236 --> 00:00:17,304
The striker goes one way,
the coin goes another.

7
00:00:17,654 --> 00:00:21,665
With two equal coins, they always
seem to split at a right angle.

8
00:00:21,665 --> 00:00:22,691
Ninety degrees.

9
00:00:22,691 --> 00:00:24,523
Why that angle, every time?

10
00:00:25,023 --> 00:00:27,489
To find out, we take
momentum into two dimensions.

11
00:00:28,089 --> 00:00:31,140
A collision in two dimensions
is a hit that is not head on.

12
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:33,309
After it, the bodies move off at angles.

13
00:00:33,659 --> 00:00:35,224
Momentum is a vector.

14
00:00:35,224 --> 00:00:38,150
So it is kept in each
direction separately.

15
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:40,507
Once along x, and once along y.

16
00:00:40,907 --> 00:00:43,958
Before the hit, coin A moves only along x.

17
00:00:43,958 --> 00:00:47,478
So the total y momentum
is zero, before and after.

18
00:00:48,078 --> 00:00:50,630
Our motion notebook, seen from above.

19
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:53,302
Coin A moves at four metres per second.

20
00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:55,924
Coin B, the same mass, is at rest.

21
00:00:56,274 --> 00:00:57,721
A hits B off centre.

22
00:00:57,721 --> 00:01:02,342
A moves off at thirty degrees above
its old line, at three point four six.

23
00:01:02,342 --> 00:01:05,180
B moves off at sixty
degrees below, at two.

24
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:09,593
Along x: three point four six
times cos thirty is three.

25
00:01:09,593 --> 00:01:11,658
Two times cos sixty is one.

26
00:01:11,658 --> 00:01:13,448
Three plus one is four.

27
00:01:13,448 --> 00:01:14,348
Kept.

28
00:01:14,438 --> 00:01:17,171
Along y: A carries plus
one point seven three.

29
00:01:17,171 --> 00:01:19,373
B carries minus one point seven three.

30
00:01:19,373 --> 00:01:20,273
They cancel.

31
00:01:20,205 --> 00:01:21,105
Zero, kept.

32
00:01:21,579 --> 00:01:22,539
Slow-mo replay.

33
00:01:22,539 --> 00:01:23,877
Freeze at the touch.

34
00:01:24,227 --> 00:01:27,488
The push acts along the line
joining the two centres.

35
00:01:27,488 --> 00:01:29,603
So B shoots off along that line.

36
00:01:30,003 --> 00:01:31,741
And A is pushed away from it.

37
00:01:31,741 --> 00:01:32,831
Thirty plus sixty.

38
00:01:32,831 --> 00:01:34,848
They split at exactly ninety degrees.

39
00:01:35,448 --> 00:01:37,667
Now aim again, closer to the edge.

40
00:01:37,667 --> 00:01:40,992
Will the angle between the
coins still be ninety degrees?

41
00:01:40,992 --> 00:01:42,142
Pause and decide.

42
00:01:45,242 --> 00:01:46,142
Yes!

43
00:01:45,723 --> 00:01:47,811
A turns by only fifteen degrees.

44
00:01:47,811 --> 00:01:49,511
B leaves at seventy five.

45
00:01:49,511 --> 00:01:51,787
Fifteen plus seventy five is ninety.

46
00:01:52,187 --> 00:01:55,016
Equal masses, an elastic hit, one at rest.

47
00:01:55,016 --> 00:01:57,221
The split is always ninety degrees.

48
00:01:57,821 --> 00:02:00,308
Now let's prove it, one step at a time.

49
00:02:00,658 --> 00:02:01,576
Step one.

50
00:02:01,576 --> 00:02:05,976
Momentum, as vectors: m u
equals m v one plus m v two.

51
00:02:05,976 --> 00:02:06,895
Cancel m.

52
00:02:06,895 --> 00:02:09,100
u equals v one plus v two.

53
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:10,750
Step two.

54
00:02:10,722 --> 00:02:15,922
Kinetic energy is kept: u squared
equals v one squared plus v two squared.

55
00:02:16,672 --> 00:02:17,638
Step three.

56
00:02:17,638 --> 00:02:22,563
Square step one: u squared equals v
one squared plus v two squared, plus

57
00:02:22,563 --> 00:02:24,253
two v one dot v two.

58
00:02:25,003 --> 00:02:25,903
Step four.

59
00:02:25,865 --> 00:02:27,083
Compare the two.

60
00:02:27,083 --> 00:02:29,499
Two v one dot v two must be zero.

61
00:02:29,499 --> 00:02:32,173
So v one is at right angles to v two.

62
00:02:32,923 --> 00:02:35,496
The three velocities
form a right triangle.

63
00:02:35,496 --> 00:02:37,932
Four, three point four six, and two.

64
00:02:38,532 --> 00:02:39,550
Remember this.

65
00:02:39,550 --> 00:02:44,044
In two dimensions, momentum is kept
along x, and along y, separately.

66
00:02:44,744 --> 00:02:46,514
Now the carrom board, link by link.

67
00:02:46,864 --> 00:02:49,195
A real striker is heavier than a coin.

68
00:02:49,595 --> 00:02:53,185
A heavy striker keeps more of
its forward motion after the hit.

69
00:02:53,585 --> 00:02:57,242
So it turns less, and the split
is smaller than ninety degrees.

70
00:02:57,242 --> 00:02:59,287
The right angle needs equal masses.

71
00:02:59,887 --> 00:03:00,878
Side by side.

72
00:03:00,878 --> 00:03:04,072
Equal coins, elastic: they
split at ninety degrees.

73
00:03:04,472 --> 00:03:08,668
A heavy striker into a light coin:
the split is less than ninety.

74
00:03:09,268 --> 00:03:10,168
Now you try.

75
00:03:10,142 --> 00:03:13,027
A light coin hits a heavy
still disc, off centre.

76
00:03:13,027 --> 00:03:15,777
Is the split less than
ninety degrees, or more?

77
00:03:15,777 --> 00:03:16,863
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:20,063 --> 00:03:21,207
More than ninety!

79
00:03:21,207 --> 00:03:25,453
The light coin turns through a big
angle, almost like a ball off a wall,

80
00:03:25,453 --> 00:03:27,240
while the disc moves forward.

81
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:29,400
Now, one worked example.

82
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:31,129
This time, the bodies stick.

83
00:03:31,479 --> 00:03:35,288
A two kilogram ball moves east
at three metres per second.

84
00:03:35,288 --> 00:03:38,317
Another two kilogram
ball moves north at four.

85
00:03:38,317 --> 00:03:39,956
They collide and stick.

86
00:03:39,956 --> 00:03:41,386
Find their velocity.

87
00:03:41,886 --> 00:03:43,847
Along x: two times three.

88
00:03:43,847 --> 00:03:46,015
Six kilogram metres per second.

89
00:03:46,765 --> 00:03:48,613
Along y: two times four.

90
00:03:48,613 --> 00:03:49,513
Eight.

91
00:03:49,972 --> 00:03:53,614
Total momentum: root of six
squared plus eight squared.

92
00:03:53,614 --> 00:03:54,514
Ten.

93
00:03:54,125 --> 00:03:55,866
Divide by four kilograms.

94
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:58,128
Two point five metres per second.

95
00:03:58,878 --> 00:04:01,455
The direction: tan
theta is eight over six.

96
00:04:01,455 --> 00:04:03,765
About fifty three degrees north of east.

97
00:04:04,415 --> 00:04:05,879
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:06,229 --> 00:04:10,177
Many students use the ninety degree rule
for every collision in two dimensions.

99
00:04:10,577 --> 00:04:15,450
It works only for equal masses, an
elastic hit, and one body at rest.

100
00:04:15,450 --> 00:04:18,031
Otherwise, solve x and y separately.

101
00:04:18,681 --> 00:04:19,854
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:19,854 --> 00:04:23,324
In glancing collisions, the ninety
degree split, and bodies that stick

103
00:04:23,324 --> 00:04:24,224
at an angle.

104
00:04:24,735 --> 00:04:25,924
Exam level now.

105
00:04:25,924 --> 00:04:30,497
A one kilogram ball at ten metres per
second hits an identical still ball

106
00:04:30,497 --> 00:04:32,240
elastically, off centre.

107
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:35,403
The first ball leaves at
six metres per second.

108
00:04:35,403 --> 00:04:40,017
Find the speed of the second ball,
and the angle between their paths.

109
00:04:40,017 --> 00:04:41,256
Pause and solve.

110
00:04:46,656 --> 00:04:47,780
The answer is B.

111
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:50,323
Eight metres per second,
at ninety degrees.

112
00:04:50,823 --> 00:04:52,595
Kinetic energy is kept.

113
00:04:52,595 --> 00:04:56,094
One hundred equals thirty
six plus v two squared.

114
00:04:56,594 --> 00:05:00,290
So v two squared is sixty
four, and v two is eight.

115
00:05:00,790 --> 00:05:03,081
Equal masses, elastic, one at rest.

116
00:05:03,081 --> 00:05:05,248
So the paths are at ninety degrees.

117
00:05:05,848 --> 00:05:07,388
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:07,388 --> 00:05:08,971
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:12,171 --> 00:05:14,109
Can you answer these without help?

120
00:05:14,509 --> 00:05:15,409
One.

121
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:18,788
Why must momentum be kept
along x and along y separately?

122
00:05:19,688 --> 00:05:20,588
Two.

123
00:05:20,079 --> 00:05:22,605
When do two coins split
at exactly ninety degrees?

124
00:05:23,505 --> 00:05:24,405
Three.

125
00:05:23,971 --> 00:05:27,517
Why does a heavy striker split from
a coin at less than ninety degrees?

126
00:05:28,417 --> 00:05:32,383
Next chapter: throw a spinning hammer,
and one point on it still flies

127
00:05:32,383 --> 00:05:33,644
in a perfect curve.

128
00:05:33,644 --> 00:05:34,544
Which point?

129
00:05:34,494 --> 00:05:36,867
System of particles and rotational motion.
